.mt-12.prose
  %h2{ id: "why", class: "scroll-mt-24" } 1. Why Fixtures
  :markdown
    A factory builds and saves its records inside every example — plus
    associations it never needed, running every callback on the way.
    Fixtures are inserted in bulk, once, and each example runs inside a
    transaction that is rolled back when it finishes.

.mt-4.overflow-x-auto.rounded-box.border.border-base-300
  = daisy_table(css: "table-zebra") do |table|
    - table.with_head do |head|
      - head.with_column { "" }
      - head.with_column { "Fixtures" }
      - head.with_column { "Factories" }
    - comparison.each do |question, fixtures, factories|
      - table.with_row do |row|
        - row.with_column { question }
        - row.with_column do
          :markdown
            #{fixtures}
        - row.with_column do
          :markdown
            #{factories}

.mt-6.prose
  :markdown
    The usual objection is that fixtures are **global**: every spec sees
    the same rows, so the record one spec needs is the record another spec
    is counting. That is true, and it is manageable. Each feature gets its
    own parent records instead of reusing shared ones, and a single spec
    proves the whole set still loads cleanly — both are in
    [Keeping a Large Set Healthy](#health).

    The other objection is readability: a factory call shows everything
    that matters on one line, while a fixture lives in YAML. So name rows
    for what they are *for* — `invoices(:billing_overdue_a_week)` tells
    the reader what the spec depends on without opening the file.

    We moved a large production Rails app from factories to fixtures, and
    these are the timings we measured along the way:

.mt-4.overflow-x-auto.rounded-box.border.border-base-300
  = daisy_table(css: "table-zebra") do |table|
    - table.with_head do |head|
      - head.with_column { "Change" }
      - head.with_column { "Before" }
      - head.with_column { "After" }
    - timings.each do |change, before, after|
      - table.with_row do |row|
        - row.with_column { change }
        - row.with_column { before }
        - row.with_column { after }

= doc_note(modifier: :tip, title: "Using Minitest?", css: "mt-6") do
  :markdown
    Fixtures are Minitest's default: a new app's `test_helper.rb` already
    calls `fixtures :all`, and the writing and health sections apply
    unchanged. We use RSpec, following
    [Debugging & Testing](#{guide_path("debugging")}), for the rest.

.mt-12.prose
  %h2{ id: "setup", class: "scroll-mt-24" } 2. Setup
  :markdown
    rspec-rails reads fixtures from the paths you give it and wraps every
    example in a transaction. List every directory that holds fixtures —
    engines and subfolders included:

.mt-6.max-w-prose
  = doc_code(language: "ruby") do
    :plain
      # spec/rails_helper.rb
      RSpec.configure do |config|
        config.use_transactional_fixtures = true

        config.fixture_paths = [
          Rails.root.join("spec/fixtures"),
          Rails.root.join("engines/billing/spec/fixtures")
        ]
      end

.mt-6.prose
  :markdown
    Then **declare fixtures per file** instead of setting
    `config.global_fixtures = :all`. Each spec names the tables it reads, so
    Rails inserts only those, and the file's dependencies are written at
    the top where a reader expects them:

.mt-6.max-w-prose
  = doc_code(language: "ruby") do
    :plain
      RSpec.describe Invoices::Overdue do
        fixtures :accounts, :customers, :invoices

        it "finds invoices a week past due" do
          expect(described_class.call).to include(invoices(:billing_overdue_a_week))
        end
      end

.mt-6.prose
  :markdown
    Loaded fixtures stay for the rest of the run, so a spec can pass by
    accident on rows an *earlier* file declared, then fail when parallel CI
    splits files differently. Random order (`config.order = :random`) and
    running each file alone both catch a missing declaration.

.mt-12.prose
  %h2{ id: "writing", class: "scroll-mt-24" } 3. Writing Fixtures
  :markdown
    A fixture file is named after its table and holds one entry per row.
    Each entry's key is its **label**, and labels are how everything else
    refers to the row:

.mt-6.max-w-prose
  = doc_code(language: "yaml") do
    :plain
      # spec/fixtures/accounts.yml
      billing_acme:
        name: Acme
        subdomain: $LABEL

      # spec/fixtures/invoices.yml
      billing_overdue_a_week:
        account: billing_acme
        total_cents: 12000
        due_on: <%= 1.week.ago.to_date %>

.mt-6.prose
  :markdown
    - **Prefix labels with their feature.** Labels must be unique within a
      table, and with dozens of contributors `acme` gets taken quickly.
      `billing_acme` and `reports_acme` never collide.
    - **Reference associations by label** (`account: billing_acme`), never
      by id. Polymorphic associations name the type too:
      `owner: billing_acme (Account)`. `$LABEL` drops a row's own label
      into a value.
    - **Never hardcode ids.** Rails derives each id from its label. When a
      spec needs the raw value — an API response, a SQL fragment — ask for
      it with `ActiveRecord::FixtureSet.identify(:billing_acme)` (pass
      `:uuid` as the second argument for UUID keys).
    - **Use ERB sparingly.** It is handy for dates and a short loop, but
      the Rails docs call dynamic values in fixtures "a code smell" — a
      fixture should read like data.
    - **Files that aren't named after their table**, such as
      `billing/plans.yml`, name their model with a top-level `_fixture:`
      entry (`model_class: Billing::Plan`) or `set_fixture_class` in the
      spec.

    Finally, **declare every table the code reads**: loading `invoices`
    does not load the `accounts` its rows point at.

.mt-12.prose
  %h2{ id: "callbacks", class: "scroll-mt-24" } 4. Callbacks and Builders
  :markdown
    Fixtures are written straight to the database in bulk SQL, so **model
    validations and callbacks never run**. Anything a callback would have
    created — a history row, a default profile, a slug — must be written
    out as fixtures of its own. That gives a simple rule:

    - **Preconditions belong in fixtures.** The account, the customer, the
      invoice that already exists when the spec starts.
    - **Records the code under test creates come from the spec.** Use a
      plain `Model.create!` for a one-off, or a small builder helper, fed by
      fixtures, when several specs need the same new record.

.mt-6.max-w-prose
  = doc_code(language: "ruby") do
    :plain
      # spec/support/builders/invoice_builder.rb
      module InvoiceBuilder
        def build_invoice(account: accounts(:billing_acme), **attributes)
          Invoice.new(account:, total_cents: 12_000,
                      due_on: 30.days.from_now, **attributes)
        end
      end

      RSpec.configure { |config| config.include InvoiceBuilder }

.mt-6.prose
  :markdown
    A builder runs your validations and callbacks, so it suits the record
    a spec is *about*. Keep it small and building on fixtures. And not
    everything a spec needs is a record. A value object or a form
    object is clearer constructed directly — `Money.new(1200, "USD")` — and
    a request spec's params are usually just a literal hash.

.mt-12.prose
  %h2{ id: "time", class: "scroll-mt-24" } 5. Dates and Time
  :markdown
    Fixture ERB runs **once, when the set first loads**, so a relative date
    like `<%= 1.week.ago.to_date %>` is relative to whenever the run
    started. That is fine for "sometime last week", and it breaks for
    anything that depends on the calendar: a month-end report, a rule that
    only applies on weekdays, a billing period boundary. A suite that is
    green on the 20th fails on the 31st, or every Monday.

    When a spec cares about the calendar, give the fixture an **absolute**
    date and freeze the clock around the spec with Rails' own `travel_to`
    (include `ActiveSupport::Testing::TimeHelpers` in your RSpec config):

.mt-6.max-w-prose
  = doc_code(language: "ruby") do
    :plain
      RSpec.describe Billing::Period do
        fixtures :accounts, :invoices

        around do |example|
          travel_to(Time.zone.local(2026, 3, 31, 9)) { example.run }
        end
      end

.mt-6.prose
  :markdown
    `travel_to` ships with Rails, so skip Timecop. If a suite already uses
    it, convert files as you touch them rather than mixing the two in one
    block — they keep separate clocks.

.mt-12.prose
  %h2{ id: "speed", class: "scroll-mt-24" } 6. Speed
  :markdown
    Fixtures remove the per-example cost of *preconditions*. What remains
    is anything expensive built on top of them — a generated statement,
    an imported report.
    [test-prof](https://test-prof.evilmartians.io/)'s `before_all` and
    `let_it_be` build those once per file, inside a transaction that is
    rolled back when the file finishes:

.mt-6.max-w-prose
  = doc_code(language: "ruby") do
    :plain
      # spec/rails_helper.rb
      require "test_prof/recipes/rspec/before_all"
      require "test_prof/recipes/rspec/let_it_be"

      # Make fixture accessors like accounts(:billing_acme) work in before_all.
      TestProf::BeforeAll.configure do |config|
        config.setup_fixtures = true
      end

      # spec/models/invoices/statement_spec.rb
      RSpec.describe Invoices::Statement do
        fixtures :accounts, :customers, :invoices

        let_it_be(:statement, refind: true) do
          Invoices::Statement.generate!(accounts(:billing_acme))
        end
      end

.mt-6.prose
  :markdown
    `refind: true` hands each example a freshly loaded copy, so one
    example's changes can't leak into the next.

    Parallel CI balances by file, so the slowest file sets the floor for
    the whole run. `bundle exec rspec --profile 20` lists the slowest
    examples and groups; split a file that dominates the list into smaller
    ones by behavior.

.mt-12.prose
  %h2{ id: "health", class: "scroll-mt-24" } 7. Keeping a Large Set Healthy
  :markdown
    Fixtures are global and additive: a row added for one spec is a row
    every other spec can see.

    **Give new rows their own parents.** Hang a new feature's invoices off
    a new `billing_acme` account, not the shared `acme`, so Acme's counts
    hold.

    **Prove the whole set coexists.** Per-file declarations mean no spec
    ever loads everything — so one spec should. Because fixtures skip
    validations, this is also the only place a fixture that breaks a
    uniqueness rule, today or on a future date, gets caught:

.mt-6.max-w-prose
  = doc_code(language: "ruby") do
    :plain
      # spec/fixtures_coexistence_spec.rb
      RSpec.describe "Fixtures" do
        fixtures :all

        def invalid_rows
          Rails.application.eager_load!
          models = ApplicationRecord.descendants.reject(&:abstract_class?)

          models.flat_map do |model|
            model.find_each.reject(&:valid?).map do |record|
              "\#{model.name} \#{record.id}: " \
                "\#{record.errors.full_messages.to_sentence}"
            end
          end
        end

        it "are all valid together" do
          expect(invalid_rows).to be_empty
        end

        it "stay valid for the coming year" do
          (1..12).each do |months|
            travel_to(months.months.from_now) { expect(invalid_rows).to be_empty }
          end
        end

        it "never repeat a label within a table" do
          labels = RSpec.configuration.fixture_paths.flat_map do |root|
            Dir["\#{root}/**/*.yml"].flat_map do |path|
              table = path.delete_prefix("\#{root}/").delete_suffix(".yml")
              File.read(path).scan(/^([a-z0-9_]+):/).flatten
                  .map { |label| "\#{table}/\#{label}" }
            end
          end

          expect(labels.tally.select { |_, count| count > 1 }.keys).to be_empty
        end
      end

.mt-6.prose
  :markdown
    The label check reads the raw files because YAML silently keeps the
    *last* of two duplicate keys. Broken foreign keys are already caught:
    on PostgreSQL and SQLite, Rails verifies them as fixtures load
    (`verify_foreign_keys_for_fixtures`, on by default since Rails 7.0).

= doc_note(modifier: :warning, title: "Fixtures Are Not Seed Data", css: "mt-6") do
  :markdown
    Don't reuse fixtures as development seeds. Fixtures are shaped for
    tests — edge cases, odd dates — and seeds for people clicking around;
    sharing them means a demo-data tweak breaks specs.

.mt-12.prose
  %h2{ id: "migrating", class: "scroll-mt-24" } 8. Coming From factory_bot
  :markdown
    An existing suite moves over **one area at a time**: a model, a
    feature, a directory. Convert how each spec gets its data and leave its
    expectations alone — a spec that tests something different after the
    change is a rewrite, not a migration.

    To keep converted specs converted, add a small custom RuboCop cop that
    flags factory calls:

.mt-6.max-w-prose
  = doc_code(language: "ruby") do
    :plain
      # lib/rubocop/cop/project/no_factories.rb
      module RuboCop
        module Cop
          module Project
            # Flags factory_bot calls so converted specs stay on fixtures.
            class NoFactories < Base
              MSG = "Use fixtures instead of factories (see the Test Fixtures guide)."

              RESTRICT_ON_SEND = %i[
                attributes_for attributes_for_list build build_list build_pair
                build_stubbed build_stubbed_list create create_list create_pair
              ].freeze

              def on_send(node)
                return unless node.first_argument&.sym_type?
                return unless node.receiver.nil? || node.receiver.source == "FactoryBot"

                add_offense(node)
              end
            end
          end
        end
      end

.mt-6.max-w-prose
  = doc_code(language: "yaml") do
    :plain
      # .rubocop.yml
      require:
        - ./lib/rubocop/cop/project/no_factories.rb

      Project/NoFactories:
        Enabled: false # CI runs it on changed spec files only
        Include:
          - "spec/**/*_spec.rb"

.mt-6.prose
  :markdown
    Disabled in the shared config, the cop stays out of editors and full
    runs. CI runs it on the spec files a change touches, since `--only`
    runs a cop even when the config disables it:

.max-w-prose
  = daisy_code(css: "my-4", prefix: "$") do
    git diff --name-only --diff-filter=AM origin/main... -- 'spec/*_spec.rb' | xargs --no-run-if-empty bundle exec rubocop --only Project/NoFactories

.prose
  :markdown
    Untouched specs stay green, and any file someone touches has to be
    converted completely — the same changed-files ratchet the
    [Component Linting](#{guide_path("component_linting")}) guide uses for
    views. Delete each factory as its last caller goes, and remove
    `factory_bot_rails` from the Gemfile when the last factory does.

= doc_note(modifier: :tip, title: "Update the Instructions Too", css: "mt-6") do
  :markdown
    In the same change as the cop, update your contributing guide, style
    guide, and AI agent instructions (`CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`). Otherwise
    people and agents keep writing factories the cop then rejects.

.mt-12.prose
  %h2{ id: "success", class: "scroll-mt-24" } Success!
  :markdown
    Your test data loads once, rolls back cheaply, and stays healthy as the
    set grows. Before you move on:

.mt-4.flex.flex-col.gap-2
